## DESCRIPTION
## Equations for lines in the plane
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## BookTitle('Algebra: Form and Function')
## BookChapter('Linear functions, expressions, and equations')
## DBsubject(Algebra)
## DBchapter(Linear equations and functions)
## DBsection(Graphs of lines)
## Date(01/01/10)
## Institution(Fort Lewis College)
## Author(Paul Pearson)
## MLT(match1)
## MLTleader(1)
## Level(2)
## TitleText1('Algebra: Form and Function')
## AuthorText1('McCallum, Connally, and Hughes-Hallett')
## EditionText1('1')
## Section1('5.4')
## Problem1('11')
## KEYWORDS('linear equations')

#  Initialization

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'plots.pl', 'parserPopUp.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

foreach my $i (0 .. 8) {
    $a[$i] = random(2, 4);
}

@functs = (
    "y = x - $a[0]",
    "-$a[1] x + $a[2] = y",
    "$a[3] = y",
    "y = -$a[4] x - $a[5]",
    "y = x + $a[6]",
    "y = \frac{x}{$a[7]}",
    "$a[8] = x"
);

$plot[$_] = Plot() for (0 .. 6);
$plot[0]->add_function("x-$a[0]",         'x', -5, 5, color => 'blue');
$plot[1]->add_function("-$a[1] x+$a[2]",  'x', -5, 5, color => 'blue');
$plot[2]->add_function("$a[3]",           'x', -5, 5, color => 'blue');
$plot[3]->add_function("-$a[4]x - $a[5]", 'x', -5, 5, color => 'blue');
$plot[4]->add_function("x+$a[6]",         'x', -5, 5, color => 'blue');
$plot[5]->add_function("x/$a[7]",         'x', -5, 5, color => 'blue');
$plot[6]->add_dataset([ $a[8], -5 ], [ $a[8], 5 ], color => 'blue');

#  Shuffle the figures

@perm      = random_subset(7, 0 .. 6);
@dropdowns = map { DropDown([ 'A' .. 'G' ], $perm[$_]) } (0 .. 6);

BEGIN_PGML
Without a calculator, match each equation with its graph A-G.

[#
    [.
        [_]{$dropdowns[0]} [` [$functs[$perm[0]]] `]
    
        [_]{$dropdowns[1]} [` [$functs[$perm[1]]] `]

        [_]{$dropdowns[2]} [` [$functs[$perm[2]]] `]

        [_]{$dropdowns[3]} [` [$functs[$perm[3]]] `]

        [_]{$dropdowns[4]} [` [$functs[$perm[4]]] `]

        [_]{$dropdowns[5]} [` [$functs[$perm[5]]] `]

        [_]{$dropdowns[6]} [` [$functs[$perm[6]]] `]
    .]

    [.
        [# [. *A* .][. *B* .][. *C* .][. *D* .]*
            [. [!!]{$plot[0]}{125}.]
            [. [!!]{$plot[1]}{125}.]
            [. [!!]{$plot[2]}{125}.]
            [. [!!]{$plot[3]}{125}.]*
           [. *E* .][. *F* .][. *G* .]*
            [. [!!]{$plot[4]}{125}.]
            [. [!!]{$plot[5]}{125}.]
            [. [!!]{$plot[6]}{125}.]
        #]{padding => 0.3}
    
        .]
#]{align => 'lc'}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
- There is one horizontal line (C) and this is [`[$functs[2]]`].
- There is one vertical line (G) and this is [`[$functs[6]]`].
- The graphs A, E and F all have positive slope. A has a [`y`]-intercept of [`-[$a[0]]`], E has a [`y`]-intercept of [$a[6]]. F has an intercept of 0.  These are [`[$functs[0]]`], [`[$functs[4]]`] and [`[$functs[5]]`] respectively.
- The graphs B and D have negative slopes.  The [`y`]-intercept of B is [$a[2]] and the [`y`]-intercept of D is [`-[$a[5]]`], so these are [`[$functs[1]]`] and [`[$functs[3]]`] respectively. 
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
